I spent two and a half weeks travelling through Croatia, and did some beer history research along the way.

That research brought together several centuries of brewing: tax records from Osijek, nineteenth-century brewery foundations, Zagreb beer halls, and the homebrewers who helped shape the country’s modern craft beer scene.

Five milestones from the Croatia timeline on Beer Chronicles:

1697 — Tax records document beer production in Tvrđavica and Varoš, settlements within present-day Osijek. They provide written evidence of local brewing long before Kajetan Šeper founded his brewery there in 1856.

1840 — Daruvar Brewery is founded on Count Janković’s estate. The brewery would become associated with the Czech brewing traditions that form part of the town’s heritage.

1892 — Zagrebačka Brewery and Malt Factory is founded. Beer production follows in 1893—a useful distinction between establishing a company and starting to brew.

December 22, 1994 — Medvedgrad opens its brewery and beer hall in Zagreb, serving beer brewed on the premises alongside food.

September 2014 — Zmajska introduces its first Pale Ale and Porter in Zagreb, connecting founder Andrej Čapka’s homebrewing background with commercial brewing.

These are five starting points for exploring a much wider history. The Croatia tag also connects stories from Karlovac, Split, Dubrovnik, Hvar, and beyond, with sources attached to each entry.

